## From Cluttered to Clean

### Without where (Cluttered)
```xiom
fn complex[K: Eq + Hash + Display, V: Clone + Display + Default, C: Ord + Clone](
  map: &Map[K, V], comparator: C
) -> Vec[(K, V)] {
  // ...
}
```

### With where (Clean)
```xiom
fn complex[K, V, C](map: &Map[K, V], comparator: C) -> Vec[(K, V)]
  where K: Eq + Hash + Display,
        V: Clone + Display + Default,
        C: Ord + Clone,
{
  // Same function, much more readable
}
```

### where with Associated Types
```xiom
fn collect_results[I](iter: I) -> Vec[I::Item]
  where I: Iterator,
        I::Item: Display,
{
  var result = Vec[I::Item]();
  for item in iter {
    io.println(item.to_str());
    result.push(item);
  }
  return result;
}
```

### Complex where Constraints
```xiom
fn generic_algorithm[T, U, F](input: &Vec[T], transform: F) -> Vec[U]
  where T: Clone + Display,
        U: Default + Display,
        F: Fn(&T) -> U,
        Vec[U]: Default,
{
  // Complex algorithm with clean signature
}
```

### When to Use where
- 3+ type parameters
- Parameters with 2+ constraints each
- Constraints involving associated types
- When the inline syntax exceeds ~80 characters

